1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,313
Vinegar goes on a salad.

2
00:00:02,313 --> 00:00:04,379
Hydrochloric acid burns skin.

3
00:00:04,379 --> 00:00:05,635
Both are acids.

4
00:00:06,035 --> 00:00:08,848
Same strength on the
label, point one molar.

5
00:00:08,848 --> 00:00:10,529
So why is one so gentle?

6
00:00:10,979 --> 00:00:13,592
Aarav reads two bottles
in the college lab.

7
00:00:13,592 --> 00:00:16,631
Point one molar acetic
acid has pH two point nine.

8
00:00:16,631 --> 00:00:19,335
Point one molar hydrochloric
acid has pH one.

9
00:00:19,685 --> 00:00:23,364
That is about seventy five times
less hydronium in the vinegar acid.

10
00:00:23,864 --> 00:00:28,215
By the end, you will know why, and you
will calculate how much a weak acid

11
00:00:28,215 --> 00:00:29,350
really breaks up.

12
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,225
Think of a class where the teacher
says, give away one toffee each.

13
00:00:34,575 --> 00:00:39,727
In a strong acid class, every student
gives a toffee, every molecule its

14
00:00:39,727 --> 00:00:40,627
proton.

15
00:00:40,861 --> 00:00:44,019
In a weak acid class,
only a few students give.

16
00:00:44,019 --> 00:00:45,589
Most keep their toffee.

17
00:00:45,989 --> 00:00:47,854
And some take toffees back.

18
00:00:47,854 --> 00:00:50,436
So a weak acid sits in an equilibrium.

19
00:00:51,036 --> 00:00:54,128
Let's put acetic acid
on our molecule bench.

20
00:00:54,478 --> 00:00:57,076
Each molecule can give
its proton to water.

21
00:00:57,076 --> 00:00:59,485
That makes hydronium and an acetate ion.

22
00:00:59,885 --> 00:01:01,580
Watch one hundred molecules.

23
00:01:01,580 --> 00:01:03,248
Only one or two break up.

24
00:01:03,248 --> 00:01:04,584
The rest stay whole.

25
00:01:04,984 --> 00:01:09,804
The fraction that breaks up is called
the degree of ionization, alpha.

26
00:01:10,204 --> 00:01:15,378
For point one molar acetic acid, alpha
is only about one point three percent.

27
00:01:15,378 --> 00:01:19,165
For hydrochloric acid, it is
almost one hundred percent.

28
00:01:19,765 --> 00:01:25,058
The equilibrium constant for this is
the acid ionization constant, K a.

29
00:01:25,408 --> 00:01:29,511
K a equals hydronium times acetate,
divided by the acid left whole.

30
00:01:29,911 --> 00:01:32,221
A bigger K a means a stronger acid.

31
00:01:32,221 --> 00:01:36,543
Hydrofluoric acid has K a three point
five times ten to the minus four.

32
00:01:36,543 --> 00:01:40,717
Hydrocyanic acid has only four point
nine times ten to the minus ten.

33
00:01:41,117 --> 00:01:46,628
Since these numbers are tiny, we
also use p K a, minus the log of K a.

34
00:01:46,628 --> 00:01:49,611
A smaller p K a means a stronger acid.

35
00:01:50,261 --> 00:01:52,435
Now let's link K a to alpha.

36
00:01:52,435 --> 00:01:53,597
Step by step.

37
00:01:53,947 --> 00:01:54,847
Step one.

38
00:01:54,744 --> 00:01:58,157
Start with c moles per
litre of acid, and no ions.

39
00:01:58,907 --> 00:01:59,807
Step two.

40
00:01:59,781 --> 00:02:02,350
At equilibrium, c alpha breaks up.

41
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:07,370
So we have c times one minus alpha
of acid, and c alpha of each ion.

42
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:09,075
Step three.

43
00:02:09,075 --> 00:02:10,195
Put these in.

44
00:02:10,195 --> 00:02:13,723
K a equals c alpha squared,
over one minus alpha.

45
00:02:14,473 --> 00:02:15,386
Step four.

46
00:02:15,386 --> 00:02:18,902
Alpha is tiny, so one
minus alpha is almost one.

47
00:02:18,902 --> 00:02:21,128
K a is about c alpha squared.

48
00:02:21,878 --> 00:02:22,778
Step five.

49
00:02:22,673 --> 00:02:25,606
So alpha equals the
square root of K a over c.

50
00:02:25,606 --> 00:02:27,252
And hydronium is c alpha.

51
00:02:28,002 --> 00:02:30,066
Weak bases work the same way.

52
00:02:30,416 --> 00:02:32,644
Ammonia takes a proton from water.

53
00:02:32,644 --> 00:02:34,815
That makes ammonium and hydroxide.

54
00:02:35,215 --> 00:02:36,946
Its constant is K b.

55
00:02:36,946 --> 00:02:42,252
For ammonia, K b is one point seven
seven times ten to the minus five.

56
00:02:42,252 --> 00:02:44,643
And p K b is minus log K b.

57
00:02:45,043 --> 00:02:49,152
An acid and the base it leaves
behind are a conjugate pair.

58
00:02:49,152 --> 00:02:52,150
For a pair, K a times K b equals K w.

59
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:58,016
Take logs, and p K a plus p K b equals
fourteen, at two ninety eight kelvin.

60
00:02:58,616 --> 00:03:02,220
Compare hydrofluoric acid and hydrocyanic
acid, at point one molar each.

61
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:03,653
Pause and think.

62
00:03:03,653 --> 00:03:06,836
At the same concentration,
which one gives more hydronium?

63
00:03:10,036 --> 00:03:11,093
Hydrofluoric acid.

64
00:03:11,093 --> 00:03:14,006
Its K a is about seven
hundred thousand times bigger.

65
00:03:14,656 --> 00:03:18,861
Some acids have more than one
proton to give, like carbonic acid.

66
00:03:19,211 --> 00:03:21,341
They give them one at a time.

67
00:03:21,341 --> 00:03:25,814
Each step has its own constant,
K a one, K a two, and so on.

68
00:03:26,214 --> 00:03:28,203
K a two is always smaller.

69
00:03:28,203 --> 00:03:33,024
Pulling a positive proton away from a
negative ion is harder, like pulling

70
00:03:33,024 --> 00:03:34,742
a magnet off a magnet.

71
00:03:35,342 --> 00:03:36,590
Remember this.

72
00:03:36,590 --> 00:03:38,862
Bigger K a, stronger acid.

73
00:03:38,862 --> 00:03:40,937
K a times K b is K w.

74
00:03:41,637 --> 00:03:43,327
Now, one worked example.

75
00:03:43,627 --> 00:03:46,577
Find the pH of point
one molar acetic acid.

76
00:03:46,577 --> 00:03:51,403
K a is one point seven four times ten
to the minus five, from the textbook

77
00:03:51,403 --> 00:03:52,303
table.

78
00:03:52,515 --> 00:03:55,453
Alpha equals the square
root of K a over c.

79
00:03:55,453 --> 00:03:59,596
Square root of one point seven
four times ten to the minus four.

80
00:04:00,346 --> 00:04:02,934
Alpha is about point zero one three two.

81
00:04:02,934 --> 00:04:05,276
That is one point three two percent.

82
00:04:06,026 --> 00:04:07,668
Hydronium equals c alpha.

83
00:04:07,668 --> 00:04:10,292
Point one times point zero one three two.

84
00:04:10,292 --> 00:04:14,333
One point three two times ten to
the minus three moles per litre.

85
00:04:15,083 --> 00:04:16,647
pH is minus log of that.

86
00:04:16,647 --> 00:04:18,332
About two point eight eight.

87
00:04:18,332 --> 00:04:20,950
Much higher than hydrochloric
acid's pH of one.

88
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:23,061
Now, the exam trap.

89
00:04:23,411 --> 00:04:26,334
Students think adding water
makes a weak acid break up less.

90
00:04:26,734 --> 00:04:28,214
It is the opposite.

91
00:04:28,214 --> 00:04:31,093
Alpha is the square root of K a over c.

92
00:04:31,093 --> 00:04:32,933
Smaller c, bigger alpha.

93
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:37,107
At point zero zero one molar,
about twelve percent breaks up.

94
00:04:37,757 --> 00:04:39,154
Where is this asked?

95
00:04:39,154 --> 00:04:41,920
Boards ask for alpha or pH of a weak acid.

96
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:44,246
Entrance tests ask for K a from pH.

97
00:04:44,846 --> 00:04:46,116
Exam level now.

98
00:04:46,116 --> 00:04:51,005
A point zero one molar solution of
a weak monobasic acid has pH four.

99
00:04:51,005 --> 00:04:52,458
What is its K a?

100
00:04:52,458 --> 00:04:53,783
Pause and solve.

101
00:04:59,183 --> 00:05:00,539
The answer is C.

102
00:05:00,539 --> 00:05:02,873
One times ten to the minus six.

103
00:05:03,373 --> 00:05:06,000
pH four means hydronium
is ten to the minus four.

104
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:08,437
K a is about hydronium squared over c.

105
00:05:08,937 --> 00:05:12,475
Ten to the minus eight,
divided by point zero one.

106
00:05:12,475 --> 00:05:14,752
One times ten to the minus six.

107
00:05:14,752 --> 00:05:16,228
A forgot to square.

108
00:05:16,828 --> 00:05:18,369
Here is your recap.

109
00:05:18,369 --> 00:05:19,952
Screenshot this card.

110
00:05:23,151 --> 00:05:25,337
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:05:25,737 --> 00:05:26,637
One.

112
00:05:26,177 --> 00:05:28,901
What does a small K a
tell you about an acid?

113
00:05:29,701 --> 00:05:30,601
Two.

114
00:05:30,087 --> 00:05:32,863
Why does a weak acid break
up more when you dilute it?

115
00:05:33,663 --> 00:05:34,563
Three.

116
00:05:34,277 --> 00:05:39,240
If p K a is four point seven six,
what is p K b of its conjugate base?

117
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:44,687
Next time: add a pinch of salt of
an acid, and the acid gets weaker.

118
00:05:44,687 --> 00:05:45,587
Why?

119
00:05:45,175 --> 00:05:47,740
And why is some salt water not neutral?

120
00:05:47,740 --> 00:05:51,339
We find out in Common Ion
Effect and Hydrolysis of Salts.
